  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 323 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nFor Friday through Sunday, guidance consensus is that weak troughing \nwill gradually diminish over the region as upper-level ridging \nbuilds into the Midwest. While a cold front associated with a system \nin southern Canada will swing south-southeastward toward the CWA, \nthe building ridge is expected to weaken the front and its \nprogression on Saturday. This front will also meet with a warm front \nlifting northeastward associated with the building ridge. Both \nfeatures will provide a medium chance (up to 50%) for showers and \nisolated thunderstorms mainly on Saturday. \n\nThe building ridge and warm front mark the start of another round of \nanomalous heat for the CWA. Low-level southwesterly flow will advect \nwarm air into the region starting Sunday and particularly Monday. \nThen, global ensemble members are tightly clustered around highs in \nthe low to mid-90s across the CWA, leading to high confidence that \nthis will be the warmest day of the period. \n\nGuidance diverges on the phasing of the ridge for Tuesday and \nbeyond, leading to high uncertainty in our local weather, \nspecifically temperatures. If the ridge holds strongly over the \nsouthern CONUS, then daily highs in the 90s will continue through \nthe middle of next week. However, if the ridge weakens, we could see \ndaily highs around 80 degrees. The current NBM and forecast attempts \nto split the difference with a front stalling across our CWA, \nleading to the warmer solution mainly for areas along and south of I-\n70, with the cooler solution favored for areas north of I-70. While \nthis is also a reasonable outcome, confidence in any solution at \nthis point is low, and the forecast for this portion of the period \nwill change in the coming days.",
